//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// A `macro_rules` call may stand where an item goes, not only inside a function body.
// Expansion parses what it produced with the same code that parses an item written by
// hand, so a macro can produce an `impl`, a `fn`, or anything else at the top level.
//
// This is what `core::num` is waiting on. `Ord` wants an impl for each of ten integer
// widths, and the `checked_*`, `wrapping_*` and `saturating_*` families want one per
// operation per width. Rust's own `core::num` is written this way; without it that module
// is thousands of hand-written lines rather than hundreds.
//
// Four things are separated here, because a macro that only ever produced one kind of
// item would pass a smaller file:
//
//   `stamp_zero`   produces an impl, and is called twice for two different types, so the
//                  expansion cannot be a single shared copy
//   `stamp_const`  produces a plain function, so items other than impls reach the parser
//   `outer`        calls `stamp_const` from inside its own expansion, so an item macro
//                  may produce another item macro call rather than only finished items
//
// `stamp_const`'s matcher writes `$name : ident $v : expr` with nothing between them, and
// that is the point rather than a style choice: `ident` takes exactly one token while
// `expr` takes every token up to the next one the matcher names. With a separator the two
// behave alike and the file would not notice `ident` losing its meaning; with none, an
// `ident` that took more would swallow the value as well as the name.
//
// `$t : ty` is the kind for the type being stamped. `expr` would capture it too, since
// those two are not told apart today, but nothing here is a type except by being used as
// one, so the kind is the only place that intent is written down.
trait Zero {
  fn zero(self : Self) -> i32;
}

macro_rules stamp_zero {
  ($t : ty) => {
    impl Zero for $t {
      fn zero(self : $t) -> i32 {
        return 0;
      }
    }
  };
}

macro_rules stamp_const {
  ($name : ident $v : expr) => {
    fn $name() -> i32 {
      return $v;
    }
  };
}

macro_rules outer {
  ($v : expr) => {
    stamp_const!(from_outer $v);
  };
}

stamp_zero!(i32);
stamp_zero!(f32);
stamp_const!(forty 40);
outer!(2);

fn main() -> i32 {
  let a : i32 = 5;
  let b : f32 = 1.5;
  print(a.zero() + b.zero());
  print!(" ");
  print(forty() + from_outer());
  return 0;
}

// EXPECT: 0 42
